In article <9404@castle.ed.ac.uk> egnr69@castle.ed.ac.uk (I Chisholm) writes:
>Monsters that should be improved on are:
>       Nazguls.... I liked all the ideas put up earlier
Me too.

>       Dragons.... I don't think it should be EVER possible to run from
>a flying monster. Resistances should make you resistant, but not proof
>against special attacks (so a fire attack would still cause you damage
>when you are fire resistant, but less than otherwise).

Well, how much flying can a dragon do in a dungeon?  I've always
imagined the rooms and tunnels being about 8-10 feet tall, maybe as
tall as 20 feet in some rooms...this doesn't seem to be enough room
for a dragon to take off.  And I imagine that the passageways/doorways
are probably FAR too narrow for the dragon to clear with its wings
stretched.  I would extend this to Nazguls, too.  I think that only
small flying creatures could actually fly in the cramped dungeon, like
bats, bees, etc.

Also, about resistance...perhaps for consistancy the breath weapon
should behave EXACTLY like a wand of the appropriate kind (and vice
versa!)  Does a player with cold resistance take any damage from a
wand of cold?  

>       Stone golems should blunt ordinary weapons.
So how do you "proof" your weapons against this?  I've always liked
that there's a neat solution to every quandry in NetHack.  What's your
solution to prevent blunting?

>       Minotaurs should have the intelligence to leg it through their
>home maze to regain Hps when they're being gubbed.
I've imagined Minotaurs as being incredible egomaniacs.  From their
mythological reputation, I thought that they had never met an enemy
that they hadn't devoured.  Why would they ever want to flee?

>While we're onto Greek mythos, how about cerberus at the gates of
>Hell: You'd find yourself in a long dark passage with the gateway at the
>far end. Cerberus would probably be a b****r to kill, needing all 3 of
>his heads to be removed cleanly to kill him. There would be no way of
>getting into hell other than past cerberus.
Hmmm.  Perhaps an "Orpheus" could charm the poor beast to sleep with
his music...?

>chis

Good suggestions, chis.

